%I A401488 #9 Nov 02 2026 22:22:28
%S A401488 1,3,10,136,32896,2147516416,9223372039002259456,
%T A401488 170141183460469231740910675752738881536,
%U A401488 57896044618658097711785492504343953926805133516280751251460479307672448925696
%N A401488 Number of Boolean functions of n variables up to duality, where f is identified with its dual f^d(x) = NOT f(NOT x).
%C A401488 Equivalently, the number of conjugation (black-white duality) classes of the 2^(2^n) binary cellular automaton rules acting on windows of n cells. The reverse complement of a rule string (in Wolfram's numbering) is the rule string of the conjugate rule. E.g., a(3) = 136 for the elementary cellular automata, of which 16 are self-conjugate.
%C A401488 Equivalently, the number of binary words of length 2^n up to reverse complement (reverse the word and exchange 0 and 1).
%C A401488 For n >= 1, the number of self-dual functions (classes with one element) is 2^(2^(n-1)) = A001146(n-1).
%C A401488 a(n) = A191363(n) for n = 1..5, because 2^(2^(n-1)) + 1 is a Fermat prime for those n. a(6) = 9223372039002259456 is not in A191363, since F_5 = 641*6700417 is composite.
%H A401488 Francisco J. Muñoz, <a href="/A401488/b401488.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..11</a>
%F A401488 a(n) = 2^(2^n-1) + 2^(2^(n-1)-1) for n >= 1.
%F A401488 a(n) = (A001146(n) + A001146(n-1))/2 for n >= 1.
%F A401488 a(n) = A000217(2^(2^(n-1))) for n >= 1.
%F A401488 a(n) = A007582(2^(n-1)) = A051437(2^n-1) = A005418(2^n+1) for n >= 1.
%e A401488 For n = 1, the four Boolean functions of one variable are 0, 1, x and NOT x. The dual of 0 is 1, while x and NOT x are self-dual. The a(1) = 3 classes are {0, 1}, {x} and {NOT x}.
%o A401488 (Python) def a(n): return 1 if n == 0 else 2**(2**n-1) + 2**(2**(n-1)-1)
%o A401488 (PARI) a(n) = if(n==0, 1, 2^(2^n-1) + 2^(2^(n-1)-1))
%Y A401488 Cf. A000217, A001146, A005418, A007582, A051437, A191363.
%K A401488 nonn,easy,new
%O A401488 0,2
%A A401488 _Francisco J. Muñoz_ and _Juan Carlos Nuño_, Oct 27 2026
